# REPLACEMENT AUTHENTICATION SPEC — MONOES.ME

Authentication for this application MUST use MonoES OAuth 2.0.

Do not implement a separate password database.

Do not introduce Auth0, Clerk, Firebase Auth, Cognito, Keycloak, Supabase Auth, or another primary identity provider.

MonoES is the identity authority.

---

# 1. MONOES AUTHORIZATION SERVER

Use the following issuer:

```text
https://monoes.me/api/auth
```

OAuth Authorization Server Metadata:

```text
GET https://monoes.me/api/auth/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server
```

At application startup, fetch and validate the authorization-server metadata where practical.

Do not hard-code metadata values that can be obtained through discovery.

The known documented endpoints are:

```text
Client registration:
POST https://monoes.me/api/auth/oauth2/register

Authorization:
GET https://monoes.me/api/auth/oauth2/authorize

Token:
POST https://monoes.me/api/auth/oauth2/token
```

---

# 2. OAUTH CLIENT REGISTRATION

MonoES supports OAuth 2.0 Dynamic Client Registration.

For development/setup, register the application with:

```bash
curl -X POST https://monoes.me/api/auth/oauth2/register \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "redirect_uris": [
      "http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/monoes"
    ],
    "token_endpoint_auth_method": "none",
    "grant_types": [
      "authorization_code",
      "refresh_token"
    ]
  }'
```

Store the returned:

```text
client_id
```

in environment configuration.

Do NOT assume a client secret exists.

The documented client configuration uses:

```text
token_endpoint_auth_method = none
```

Therefore implement Authorization Code + PKCE as the primary browser login mechanism.

For production registration use the real HTTPS callback URL.

Example:

```text
https://app.example.com/api/auth/callback/monoes
```

Never register wildcard redirect URIs.

---

# 3. ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES

Use:

```env
MONOES_ISSUER=https://monoes.me/api/auth

MONOES_CLIENT_ID=

MONOES_AUTHORIZATION_URL=https://monoes.me/api/auth/oauth2/authorize
MONOES_TOKEN_URL=https://monoes.me/api/auth/oauth2/token

MONOES_METADATA_URL=https://monoes.me/api/auth/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server

MONOES_SCOPES="openid profile email"

MONOES_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/monoes
```

Do not require:

```env
MONOES_CLIENT_SECRET
```

unless future MonoES configuration explicitly introduces a confidential-client authentication method.

Prefer values discovered from RFC 8414 metadata over duplicated environment configuration when possible.

---

# 4. LOGIN FLOW

Implement OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code Flow with PKCE.

When:

```text
GET /api/auth/login
```

is called:

Generate cryptographically secure:

```text
state
code_verifier
code_challenge
```

Use:

```text
code_challenge_method=S256
```

Store state and verifier temporarily in a secure HttpOnly login transaction cookie or equivalent server-side transaction state.

Generate authorization URL conceptually as:

```text
https://monoes.me/api/auth/oauth2/authorize
  ?client_id=<MONOES_CLIENT_ID>
  &redirect_uri=<MONOES_REDIRECT_URI>
  &response_type=code
  &scope=openid+profile+email
  &code_challenge=<CODE_CHALLENGE>
  &code_challenge_method=S256
  &state=<STATE>
```

Redirect the browser there.

Do not use OAuth implicit flow.

---

# 5. CALLBACK

Implement:

```text
GET /api/auth/callback/monoes
```

Read:

```text
code
state
```

Verify returned state exactly matches the login transaction.

Reject:

```text
missing state
incorrect state
missing authorization code
expired login transaction
reused transaction
```

Then exchange the authorization code against:

```text
POST https://monoes.me/api/auth/oauth2/token
```

Use:

```text
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
```

Submit:

```text
grant_type=authorization_code
code=<authorization code>
redirect_uri=<configured redirect URI>
client_id=<MonoES client ID>
code_verifier=<original PKCE verifier>
```

Do not send a fabricated client secret.

---

# 6. SCOPES

For authentication into our avatar application, initially request:

```text
openid
profile
email
```

Do NOT request:

```text
community:read
community:write
```

unless the avatar product genuinely needs access to the MonoES Community API.

Use least privilege.

MonoES documents the following scope classes:

```text
openid
profile
email

community:read
community:write

offline_access
```

`community:write` does NOT imply `community:read`.

Never assume scope inheritance.

---

# 7. REFRESH TOKENS

Do NOT request `offline_access` for the initial MVP unless persistent provider access is actually required.

If long-lived MonoES API access becomes necessary, add:

```text
offline_access
```

to the authorization scope.

MonoES only returns a refresh token when `offline_access` is explicitly requested.

Registering:

```text
grant_types = ["authorization_code", "refresh_token"]
```

alone is NOT enough to obtain one.

If refresh tokens are enabled:

- encrypt them at rest;
- never expose them to browser JavaScript;
- rotate/revoke local sessions appropriately;
- avoid logging them.

---

# 8. ACCESS TOKEN PURPOSE

Treat a MonoES access token as an authorization credential for MonoES resources.

Do NOT use the raw MonoES access token as our application's long-lived browser session.

After successful login:

1. validate the MonoES OAuth response;
2. resolve the user's external identity;
3. create/update the local User;
4. create our own application session;
5. issue a secure HttpOnly application-session cookie.

This keeps MonoES authentication separate from our platform session lifecycle.

---

# 9. USER IDENTITY

Use the stable provider identity supplied by MonoES/OIDC rather than email whenever available.

Create:

```text
ExternalIdentity

id
userId
provider
providerSubject
email
profileMetadata
createdAt
updatedAt
```

Set:

```text
provider = "monoes"
```

Use the stable OAuth/OIDC subject identifier as:

```text
providerSubject
```

Unique constraint:

```text
(provider, providerSubject)
```

Email is profile data.

Do NOT use email as the primary external identity key.

---

# 10. LOCAL USER

Create:

```text
User

id
email
displayName
avatarUrl
createdAt
updatedAt
lastLoginAt
```

On first login:

1. exchange authorization code;
2. obtain/validate identity;
3. create User;
4. create ExternalIdentity;
5. create default Organization;
6. create OWNER OrganizationMember;
7. create application Session;
8. redirect to `/dashboard`.

On subsequent login:

1. locate ExternalIdentity by provider + providerSubject;
2. update safe profile attributes;
3. update lastLoginAt;
4. create/rotate application Session;
5. redirect to `/dashboard`.

Never create duplicate users just because the user's email changed.

---

# 11. APPLICATION SESSION

Create:

```text
Session

id
userId
sessionTokenHash
createdAt
expiresAt
lastSeenAt
revokedAt
userAgent
ipHash
```

Send only an opaque session token to the browser.

Store a hash of the token in PostgreSQL.

Production cookie:

```text
HttpOnly=true
Secure=true
SameSite=Lax
Path=/
```

Development may allow:

```text
Secure=false
```

only when running over localhost HTTP.

Never store the session token in:

```text
localStorage
sessionStorage
```

---

# 12. AUTH API

Implement:

```text
GET  /api/auth/login
GET  /api/auth/callback/monoes
POST /api/auth/logout
GET  /api/auth/session
GET  /api/auth/me
```

Optional developer endpoint:

```text
GET /api/auth/debug
```

must only exist in development mode and must never expose tokens or secrets.

---

# 13. LOGOUT

Application logout must:

1. revoke/delete the local application session;
2. expire the browser cookie;
3. redirect to an appropriate logged-out page.

If MonoES metadata exposes a standardized provider logout endpoint, support provider logout separately.

Do not invent one.

